    "dictionary-key": {
      "$comment": "Scans a dictionary entry forward from its own boundary instead of backward from its separator, so a key of any shape hides the type-shaped token that follows the ':' from the generic #type-positions rule. Per GRAMMAR.md a Python-style entry's key is an arbitrary expression, which no bounded lookbehind can describe; the region therefore opens at an entry boundary (the start of a line inside the dictionary, or right after '{' or ',') and closes on the first ':' that is not nested inside a group. Nesting is handled structurally rather than by counting: #dictionary-parentheses, #dictionary-brackets, and #dictionary-literal consume balanced '(...)', '[...]', and '{...}' regions before the 'end' pattern can see anything inside them, so a ',' or ':' belonging to a call argument list, a subscript, or a lambda signature cannot terminate the key early. The 'end' pattern also bails out at an unconsumed ',' or '}', and unconditionally at the end of the physical line, so a region that never meets a separator closes where it started rather than running on: a Lua-style 'key = value' entry -- whose separator this rule deliberately does not match, because a plain reassignment inside a nested lambda body is indistinguishable from one -- and an identifier-led statement in such a body both end on their own line instead of consuming the ':' of the line below. A key genuinely continued across a line break therefore reopens on the continuation line, which still keeps the separator's value from reaching #type-positions. The trailing type-shaped token is only consumed when it is not itself the callable of a constructor/conversion call (e.g. 'Vector2()'), which #calls must keep scoping normally; the whitespace after ':' is consumed unconditionally so #type-positions cannot start a match there. #contextual-keywords is left out so an accessor-shaped key such as 'get' stays an ordinary identifier. A '@' is excluded from the boundary for the same reason a statement keyword is: an annotation is not an expression, so a same-line annotated declaration inside a nested lambda body ('@warning_ignore(...) var result: Player') is a statement rather than an entry. The '(?!(?:breakpoint|class_name|trait_name|tuple_name|enum_name|namespace|abstract|continue|extends|assert|import|return|signal|static|break|class|const|final|match|trait|tuple|while|yield|elif|else|enum|pass|uses|void|when|and|for|var|as|if|in|is|or)\\b)' lookahead keeps the boundary from firing on a statement inside a nested lambda body -- a typed local such as 'var result: Player' would otherwise read as a key and swallow its own type -- by admitting only the reserved words that can begin an expression.",
